DOCUMENT 1
Session Summary & Release Notes
Midas_V2 — v0.8.1.2.0 (Documentation-Only Version)
Use this file at the start of the next session to restore full context.
1. What experiment this version documents

Version v0.8.1.2.0 documents a payoff-geometry experiment conducted on Scenario B (Gap-and-Go).

The experiment tested whether tightening the fixed stop-loss from 2.5% → 2.0%, while keeping the take-profit fixed at 2.0%, would improve profitability by:

Improving payoff symmetry

Lowering the theoretical break-even win rate

Reducing downside damage on losing days

The experiment was intentionally minimal and isolated:

Only one parameter was changed (stop-loss percentage)

No changes were made to signals, filters, sizing, exits, or universe logic

The VWAP Extension Gate remained ON throughout

This document records the hypothesis, execution, results, and final decision.

2. Purpose of this version

The purpose of v0.8.1.2.0 is purely documentary.

No production logic or configuration changes are introduced or retained.

This version exists to:

Preserve the exact hypothesis that was tested

Record the experimental design and results

Capture the reasoning behind rejecting the change

Prevent future re-testing of a disproven idea

Provide a clean, factual handover to the next version

This version should be treated as a decision checkpoint, not a strategy update.

3. Confirmed baseline at the start of the session

At the beginning of this session, Scenario B had the following verified baseline configuration:

Strategy: Scenario B – Gap-and-Go

VWAP Extension Gate: ON

vwap_extension_gate = true

vwap_extension_max_pct = 1.5

Take-profit: +2.0%

Stop-loss: −2.5%

Gate minutes: 20

RVOL open filter: min_rvol_open = 2.0

MACD rising bars: 2

No catalysts

No adaptive sizing

No dynamic exits

No additional microstructure gating

This baseline had already demonstrated:

Clear improvement over pre-VWAP-extension behavior

Reduced over-extended chase entries

Strong performance on genuine momentum days (e.g. 2025-08-06)

This configuration was treated as stable and correct going into the experiment.

4. Hypothesis tested

Hypothesis

Tightening the stop-loss from 2.5% to 2.0%, while keeping take-profit fixed at 2.0%, will improve overall profitability by improving payoff symmetry and reducing required break-even win rate.

Why this hypothesis was reasonable to test

VWAP-disciplined entries suggested trades might require less downside room

A symmetric 2.0 / 2.0 payoff lowers theoretical break-even win rate

The change was a single-parameter adjustment suitable for a clean A/B test

This was explicitly a payoff-geometry experiment, not a signal, filter, or regime experiment.

5. What was changed temporarily during the experiment
Files involved

During the experiment, the following file was temporarily modified:

config/scenarios.json

Scenario B field:

"sl_pct": 2.5 → "sl_pct": 2.0

No other fields were modified.

Files explicitly NOT changed

src/midas_v2/strategy.py

Verified that sl_pct is correctly read from scenario configuration

No logic changes were required or made

No runner scripts were modified

No data ingestion scripts were modified

No sizing or risk logic was modified

This ensured the experiment tested geometry only, not behavior.

6. Experiment design (controlled A/B test)

Scenario: B

VWAP Extension Gate: ON

Dates tested: 2025-08-05 → 2025-08-09 (weekend auto-skipped)

Universe: daily Top-5 gappers

All logic identical except stop-loss percentage

Command used
python scripts\run_range_and_summarize.py --start 2025-08-05 --end 2025-08-09 --scenario B


Two variants were tested back-to-back on the same dates and universe.

7. Results
Variant A — Tight stop (experimental)

TP = 2.0%

SL = 2.0%

Totals

Trades: 14

Wins / Losses: 5 / 9

Win rate: 35.71%

Total PnL: −140.17

Observed behavior

Trades were stopped out earlier

Several trades later recovered but were already exited

Weak days produced large clustered losses

Volatility and instability increased

Variant B — Baseline stop (control)

TP = 2.0%

SL = 2.5%

Totals

Trades: 15

Wins / Losses: 8 / 7

Win rate: 53.33%

Total PnL: −21.31

Observed behavior

Trades had sufficient room to develop

Losses on weak days were materially smaller

Strategy behavior was more stable and realistic

8. Decision

The experiment is rejected.

Tightening the stop-loss to 2.0%:

Materially reduced win rate

Increased volatility

Worsened total PnL

Did not meaningfully reduce worst-day damage

Even with VWAP-disciplined entries, Scenario B requires more than 2% downside room.

9. Restoration of baseline

After completing the A/B test:

config/scenarios.json was restored to:

"sl_pct": 2.5

No other files required restoration

A final single-day verification run was executed on 2025-08-06, confirming:

Stop-loss = 2.5%

Take-profit = 2.0%

VWAP Extension Gate active

Behavior consistent with pre-experiment baseline

This confirms the system is fully restored to its prior working state.

10. Final baseline decision

The following configuration is explicitly reaffirmed as the Scenario B baseline:

Stop-loss = 2.5%

Take-profit = 2.0%

VWAP Extension Gate = ON

This baseline remains the reference point for all future work.

11. Key insight from this session

The primary profitability bottleneck is not payoff geometry.
It is participation on the wrong days.

Evidence:

Strong days remain profitable with current logic

Weak days (e.g. 2025-08-07, 2025-08-08) generate clustered losses

Geometry changes alone cannot fix poor day selection

This reframes the problem from “how to manage trades better” to
“when not to trade at all.”

12. Why this is a documentation-only version

The experiment produced a clear negative result

No production change was adopted

All temporary changes were reverted

The value lies in the decision and reasoning, not code changes

Version v0.8.1.2.0 therefore exists to:

Preserve the tested hypothesis

Preserve the experimental evidence

Prevent regression into re-testing a disproven idea

Provide a clean conceptual handover to the next version

13. Implication for the next version

The next profitability lever must operate at the day level, not the trade level.

Filtering bad days — not tightening stops — is the correct next direction.

This directly motivates the proposed Day Follow-Through Gate, detailed in the handover document.